  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a simplified framework for shifting consumption and production toward a slower, more circular economy by showing four key groups: policymakers, industry, retailers, and consumers. Each group is illustrated with an icon and a short set of actions, emphasizing that change depends on coordinated efforts across the whole system rather than one actor alone.\n\nOn the policy side, the image highlights legislation, regulation, green taxation, and extended producer responsibility as tools for steering markets toward better balance and reduced speed. Industry is shown as responsible for operational changes such as preventing waste, investing in pollution-control technology, avoiding overproduction, improving supply-chain transparency, and closing material loops. Retailers are positioned as intermediaries who can reshape demand through new business models and pricing systems that account for environmental impact and encourage slower consumption. Consumers are depicted as supporting the transition by extending product lifetimes, consuming more consciously, and buying less frequently.\n\nOverall, the figure’s main message is that environmental improvement comes from systemic change across governance, production, sales, and use, with all actors contributing to reduced waste and longer product life cycles.",
